#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120013 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120013 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0ef52fb7-e1f4-5106-bb36-6b94715ae636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120641 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120641 #edb/ref 1061 626149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120641 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c3443d00-c515-5a63-93a0-747d76d2271c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120641 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120642 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120642 #edb/ref 1061 626150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120642 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c9c3f801-f35a-5089-8d66-01d0fd9903d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120642 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120643 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120643 #edb/ref 1061 626153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120643 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "812571c6-b194-5ab1-b16a-bcf2ac6ffbe9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120643 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120644 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120644 #edb/ref 1061 626156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120644 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ee849a7a-e449-5317-872e-183c46fabb72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120644 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120645 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120645 #edb/ref 1061 626157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120645 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8e583ccb-952c-52c6-b8a9-e4b716f0d3c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120645 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120646 #edb/ref 1061 626160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120646 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1aa2d247-5dd4-5fdf-a44a-9cf0a8cd1e0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120646 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121148 #edb/ref 1064 "The **principal normal vector** to a curve $C$ at a point $P$ is a unit vector that \n\n- is perpendicular to the unit tangent vector, and\n\n- points in the direction that the curve is bending.\n\nIn general, if the unit tangent vector is given by $\\mathbf T(t),$ then the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|},\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that $\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|\\neq 0.$\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that the curve $C$ is parametrized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\cos t, \\: 2\\sin t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0, \\pi].\n$$\n\nWe will compute the principal normal vector at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\nFirst, using the formula for the unit tangent, $\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf f'(t)}{||\\mathbf f'(t)||},$ we find that the general unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)= \\left\\langle {-\\sin t}, \\cos t\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNow, when $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we have the point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ The unit tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and the principal normal vector $\\mathbf{N}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ at this point are shown below:\n\n![](images/d0/d0a30a44bb825a8a12ca54153b6223b9bf0c2b754245b21650a5664db5e1647e.png)\n\nTo calculate the principal normal vector, we start by calculating the derivative of the unit tangent function. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general principal normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{||{T}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $\\mathbf N\\left(\\dfrac\\pi 4\\right),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, it's easy to check that $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and $\\mathbf N'(t)$ are perpendicular for all $t$ by computing the dot product and showing that we get zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\cdot N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121148 #edb/ref 1065 9376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121148 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "03709847-4a63-5975-b20a-1d6693ceadb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121148 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121149 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $\\mathbf T(t)$ be the unit tangent vector to some differentiable vector-valued function. Since $||\\mathbf T(t)|| = 1,$ and $\\mathbf T(t)$ is obviously parallel to itself, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) \\cdot \\mathbf T(t) = 1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above relationship with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T'(t) \\cdot \\mathbf T(t) + \\mathbf T(t) \\cdot \\mathbf T'(t) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t)\\cdot T(t)+{T}^{′}(t)\\cdot T(t) & =0 \\\\ 2{T}^{′}(t)\\cdot T(t) & =0 \\\\ {T}^{′}(t)\\cdot T(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, provided that $\\mathbf T'(t)\\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then it must be the case that $\\mathbf T'(t)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathbf T(t).$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf T'(t)$ measures the rate of change of $\\mathbf T(t).$ However, since $\\mathbf T(t)$ is a unit vector for all $t,$ it changes only in its direction. The vector $\\mathbf T'(t)$ measures this rate of change in direction. \n\nTo form the principal normal $\\mathbf N(t),$ we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t),$ which leads to the definition\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{||\\mathbf T'(t)||}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121149 #edb/ref 1065 9377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121149 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bd9190b9-f59c-5c06-987a-c6269ec9b3a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121149 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Formula for the Principal Normal" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121150 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the straight line given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t\\,[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf we attempt to calculate its principal normal vector, we will get an interesting result. First, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\begin{aligned}-2+5t \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=\\sqrt{27}=3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\,[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the case of a straight line, $\\mathbf T(t)$ is simply the (normalized) direction vector, as we would expect. \n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\,[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}]=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{T}'(t)$ is the zero vector, we can't normalize it as usual. If we try to apply the usual formula we get an undefined result due to division by zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{0}{‖0‖}=\\frac{0}{0}=\\text{undefined}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a straight line does not have a principal normal.\n\nThe intuition behind this result is that the principal normal points in the direction in which a curve is bending, but a straight line does not bend at all." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121150 #edb/ref 1065 9378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121150 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5c27df4b-0d17-5407-997b-c240d492eeca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121150 #edb/ref 1067 "Lines Do Not Have Principal Normal Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288333 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{sin}(t+1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288333 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288333 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d617821-6685-519e-9a4d-28281ac958a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186288333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289935 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289935 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289935 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289935 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5ee93b5a-7a7f-5de6-b120-52987f2cfbac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290399 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290399 #edb/ref 1069 "q-223101" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290399 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290399 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,k).\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$k$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290399 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "705b4f99-555b-54e9-8531-2285040b4156" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290399 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}‖\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k)}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342695 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342695 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342695 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "375ef518-4d6a-56b5-8701-cd9802686411" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342784 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,0,3⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342784 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342784 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "41e5f4d8-856b-52f5-85b3-07ac865cef2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343141 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,0,-1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343141 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343141 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "69d5fca1-eec8-5d54-b1fc-d0f9751e2efa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343245 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,1,-1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343245 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343245 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "745b8d3b-d175-5c42-a346-9db9b829c2bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343779 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,1,0⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343779 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343779 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "af1ce442-73ac-5780-9670-756b226a0a89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344702 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186342695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344702 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186342784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344702 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344702 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344702 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344702 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344702 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344702 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186343779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344702 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "74402250-c07b-5643-a659-e21ce9c35c06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345091 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345091 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345091 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345091 #edb/ref 1071 "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨3,0,-4⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345091 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b15f4c73-c376-5fbd-b671-915cb2fd0f2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345091 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{5}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398326 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398326 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398326 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398326 #edb/ref 1071 "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=-2\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398326 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d57e43b-681d-5aa1-a53c-acdd85847d41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398326 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{2(0)}{({0}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =0(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k)+\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-j+k}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-j\\cdot j+k\\cdot k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-1+1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398327 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398